## Why it exists
A single `server.js` works for a demo. A real API has to stay readable at 50 endpoints, be testable without a network, and shut down cleanly during a deploy. A small amount of structure solves all three.

{{dg:0}}

## Folder layout
```text
src/
  app.js            # builds the Express app (no listen)
  server.js         # starts it and handles shutdown
  config.js         # reads and validates env vars
  routes/orders.js
  services/orders.js
  repos/orders.js
  middleware/
test/orders.test.js
```
Keeping `app.js` separate from `server.js` lets tests import the app without opening a port.

## A repository with a connection pool
```js
import pg from 'pg'
export const pool = new pg.Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL, max: 10 })

export async function findOrder(id) {
  const { rows } = await pool.query('select id, status, total_cents from orders where id = $1', [id])
  return rows[0] ?? null
}

export async function createOrderWithItems(customerId, items) {
  const client = await pool.connect()
  try {
    await client.query('begin')
    const { rows } = await client.query(
      'insert into orders (customer_id, status) values ($1, $2) returning id', [customerId, 'pending'])
    for (const it of items) {
      await client.query('insert into order_items (order_id, sku, qty) values ($1, $2, $3)', [rows[0].id, it.sku, it.qty])
    }
    await client.query('commit')
    return rows[0].id
  } catch (e) {
    await client.query('rollback')
    throw e
  } finally {
    client.release()
  }
}
```
Always use `$1` placeholders: values travel separately from the SQL text, which prevents injection. Always `release()` the client in `finally`, or the pool runs dry.

## Cursor pagination
```js
// GET /orders?after=<id>&limit=20
const { rows } = await pool.query(
  'select id, status from orders where id < $1 order by id desc limit $2',
  [after ?? '9223372036854775807', limit + 1])
const hasMore = rows.length > limit
res.json({ items: rows.slice(0, limit), next: hasMore ? rows[limit - 1].id : null })
```
Cursor pagination stays fast on deep pages because it uses an index seek instead of skipping rows with OFFSET.

## Graceful shutdown
```js
const server = app.listen(port)
for (const sig of ['SIGTERM', 'SIGINT']) {
  process.on(sig, () => {
    server.close(async () => { await pool.end(); process.exit(0) })
    setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10_000).unref()   // force exit if stuck
  })
}
```
Containers send SIGTERM before killing a process. Finishing in-flight requests first prevents dropped connections during deploys.

## Testing
```js
import request from 'supertest'
import { test, expect } from 'vitest'
import { app } from '../src/app.js'

test('rejects an invalid email', async () => {
  const res = await request(app).post('/users').send({ email: 'nope', name: 'Ana' })
  expect(res.status).toBe(422)
})
```
Use fast unit tests for services, a handful of integration tests against a real Postgres (Docker or Testcontainers), and very few end-to-end tests.

> [!pitfall] Creating a new pg.Client per request exhausts database connections. Use one shared pool, and size it so instances times pool max stays under the database connection limit.
> [!tip] Return one consistent error shape everywhere, such as error, message and requestId, so clients handle failures uniformly.
> [!prod] With serverless or many instances, put a pooler (PgBouncer or Supabase's pooler) between the app and Postgres.

node-express-internals.md:
## Why it exists

Most Express confusion has the same few causes: "why does my route never run?", "why did the response hang?", "why did my error handler not fire?", "why is this header missing on one route?". They all stop being mysterious once you see what Express actually is. **Express is a list of functions and a loop that calls them in order.** That is almost all of it. Routing, body parsing, authentication, logging, CORS and error handling are just functions in that list.

The best way to see this is to build it. In about 50 lines on top of Node's built-in `http` module we can implement the heart of Express (`app.use`, `app.get`, `app.post`, route parameters, `next()`, error-handling middleware, promise-aware handlers) and run real HTTP requests through it. Then you will know exactly what the real library is doing when you write the same code against Express itself. (Your existing Express lessons cover Express 5 usage; this one explains why it behaves as it does.)

**Mental model:** an assembly line. A request arrives at the start of the line. Each station (middleware) can do one of four things: **modify** the request and pass it on (`next()`), **finish** the job by sending a response (the line stops here), **raise an alarm** (`next(err)`, which skips all normal stations and goes to the next *error* station), or **do nothing** (the request is stuck forever and the client waits). Stations are visited **in the order they were installed**.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

### Node's raw material: `http` and the stream of a request

Node's `http.createServer(handler)` calls `handler(req, res)` for every request. `req` is a readable stream (headers are available at once; the body arrives in chunks), `res` is a writable stream (set the status and headers, then `res.end(body)`). With nothing else you have to parse the URL, choose what to do by hand, read and parse the body yourself and catch every error. A framework exists to organise exactly that.

### The mini-Express

```javascript
import http from "node:http";

export default function miniExpress() {
  const stack = []; // every app.use / app.get call adds one layer, in order

  function compile(path) {                       // "/users/:id" -> /^\/users\/([^/]+)$/ and ["id"]
    const names = [];
    const source = path.replace(/:([A-Za-z_]+)/g, (_, name) => { names.push(name); return "([^/]+)"; });
    return { regex: new RegExp(`^${source}$`), names };
  }

  function app(req, res) {
    const url = new URL(req.url, "http://localhost");
    req.path = url.pathname;
    req.query = Object.fromEntries(url.searchParams);
    req.params = {};
    res.status = (code) => { res.statusCode = code; return res; };
    res.json = (body) => { res.setHeader("content-type", "application/json"); res.end(JSON.stringify(body)); };

    let index = 0;
    function next(err) {
      const layer = stack[index++];
      if (!layer) {                                // fell off the end of the stack
        if (err) return res.status(500).end("Internal Server Error");
        return res.status(404).end(`Cannot ${req.method} ${req.path}`);
      }
      if (layer.method && layer.method !== req.method) return next(err);
      if (layer.regex) {
        const match = layer.regex.exec(req.path);
        if (!match) return next(err);
        layer.names.forEach((name, i) => (req.params[name] = match[i + 1]));
      }
      const isErrorHandler = layer.handler.length === 4;   // (err, req, res, next)
      if (err && !isErrorHandler) return next(err);        // skip normal handlers while an error is travelling
      if (!err && isErrorHandler) return next();           // skip error handlers when nothing went wrong
      try {
        const result = err ? layer.handler(err, req, res, next) : layer.handler(req, res, next);
        if (result && typeof result.then === "function") result.catch(next);  // forward rejected promises (as Express 5 does)
      } catch (thrown) {
        next(thrown);
      }
    }
    next();
  }

  app.use = (handler) => stack.push({ handler });
  for (const verb of ["get", "post", "put", "delete"]) {
    app[verb] = (path, ...handlers) => handlers.forEach((handler) => stack.push({ method: verb.toUpperCase(), ...compile(path), handler }));
  }
  app.listen = (port, callback) => http.createServer(app).listen(port, callback);
  return app;
}
```

Read it in five pieces:

1. **The stack.** `app.use(fn)` and `app.get(path, ...fns)` push *layers* onto an array in the order you call them. A layer is a function plus an optional HTTP method and path pattern. This array **is** the application.
2. **`compile`** turns a path such as `/users/:id` into a regular expression with capture groups and remembers the parameter names. That is the essence of Express's route matching (the real one uses the `path-to-regexp` library).
3. **`app(req, res)`** is the request handler. It decorates `req` and `res` (`req.path`, `req.query`, `res.json`) and defines `next`, which **walks the stack with an index**. A layer that does not match the method or path is skipped, and a matching one is called with `(req, res, next)`.
4. **Error handling.** If something calls `next(err)`, the loop keeps walking but **only stops at functions that declare four parameters** `(err, req, res, next)`. Express looks at `function.length`, exactly like line `layer.handler.length === 4` above. That is why an error handler with only three parameters never fires, a classic bug. While an error is in flight, normal handlers are skipped, and when nothing is wrong, error handlers are skipped.
5. **Falling off the end.** If the loop runs out of layers with no error, nobody answered: that is the `404`. If it runs out *with* an error, nobody handled it: that is the default `500`. Real Express has the same final handler, and shows the stack trace only when `NODE_ENV` is not `production`.

Two details matter in practice: the `try/catch` turns a **synchronous throw** into `next(err)`, and the check on a returned promise turns a **rejected promise** into `next(err)` too. Express 5 does the latter for you (rejected promises from handlers and middleware are forwarded to the error handler, as its documentation and migration guides describe). Express 4 did not, which is why Express 4 code is full of `try { ... } catch (e) { next(e) }`.

### A demo application using every feature

```javascript
import miniExpress from "./mini-express.mjs";

const trace = [];
const app = miniExpress();

app.get("/early", (req, res) => res.json({ note: "registered BEFORE the header middleware" }));

app.use((req, res, next) => {                       // runs for every request registered after it
  res.setHeader("x-served-by", "mini-express");
  next();
});

app.use((req, res, next) => {                       // logger: note what happens after next() returns
  trace.push(`> ${req.method} ${req.path}`);
  res.on("finish", () => trace.push(`< ${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode}`));
  next();
});

app.use((req, res, next) => {                       // JSON body parser
  if (!["POST", "PUT"].includes(req.method)) return next();
  let raw = "";
  req.on("data", (chunk) => (raw += chunk));
  req.on("end", () => {
    try {
      req.body = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : {};
      next();
    } catch {
      next(Object.assign(new Error("invalid JSON body"), { status: 400 }));
    }
  });
});

const requireToken = (req, res, next) =>
  req.headers.authorization === "Bearer letmein" ? next() : res.status(401).json({ error: "missing or bad token" });

const validateUser = (req, res, next) =>
  typeof req.body?.name === "string" && req.body.name ? next() : res.status(422).json({ error: "name is required" });

app.get("/users/:id", (req, res) => res.json({ id: req.params.id, query: req.query }));
app.post("/users", validateUser, (req, res) => res.status(201).json({ created: req.body.name }));
app.get("/admin", requireToken, (req, res) => res.json({ secret: "admin data" }));
app.get("/boom", () => { throw new Error("sync failure"); });
app.get("/boom-async", async () => { throw new Error("async failure"); });

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {                  // error handler: four arguments, registered last
  res.status(err.status ?? 500).json({ error: err.message });
});

const server = app.listen(0, async () => {
  const base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;
  const call = async (label, path, options) => {
    const res = await fetch(base + path, options);
    const text = (await res.text()).trim();
    console.log(`${label.padEnd(34)} ${String(res.status)}  ${text}   [x-served-by: ${res.headers.get("x-served-by")}]`);
  };
  const json = (body) => ({ method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json" }, body });

  await call("GET /early", "/early");
  await call("GET /users/42?verbose=1", "/users/42?verbose=1");
  await call("POST /users {name}", "/users", json('{"name":"Ada"}'));
  await call("POST /users {} (validation)", "/users", json("{}"));
  await call("POST /users not json", "/users", json("{oops"));
  await call("GET /admin no token", "/admin");
  await call("GET /admin with token", "/admin", { headers: { authorization: "Bearer letmein" } });
  await call("GET /boom (sync throw)", "/boom");
  await call("GET /boom-async (rejected promise)", "/boom-async");
  await call("GET /nope", "/nope");
  console.log("\nrequest trace (order of middleware and finish events, first 8):");
  trace.slice(0, 8).forEach((line) => console.log("  " + line));
  server.close();
});
```

