GET /boom (sync throw)             500  {"error":"sync failure"}   [x-served-by: mini-express]
GET /boom-async (rejected promise) 500  {"error":"async failure"}   [x-served-by: mini-express]
GET /nope                          404  Cannot GET /nope   [x-served-by: mini-express]

request trace (order of middleware and finish events, first 8):
  > GET /users/42
  < GET /users/42 200
  > POST /users
  < POST /users 201
  > POST /users
  < POST /users 422
  > POST /users
  < POST /users 400
```

Every line of the transcript demonstrates a rule you will meet in real Express:

- **`/early` has no `x-served-by` header; every later route does.** The route was registered **before** the middleware that sets the header, so the loop answered it and never reached that middleware. Order of registration is order of execution. This is why `app.use(cors())`, `app.use(express.json())` and authentication belong **above** the routes they protect.
- **`/users/42?verbose=1`** shows parameters (`req.params.id` is `"42"`, always a string) and the parsed query string.
- **`422` and `401`:** `validateUser` and `requireToken` answer the request themselves and **do not call `next()`**. That is how a middleware "blocks" a route. The route handler after it never runs.
- **`400` for the broken JSON:** the body parser caught the `JSON.parse` failure and called `next(error)`. All normal layers were skipped and the **four-argument** handler at the bottom produced the JSON error. One centralised error handler gives every failure the same response shape.
- **`/boom` and `/boom-async` both gave `500`** with their own messages: the synchronous throw and the rejected promise were both converted to `next(err)`.
- **`404` text** is the "fell off the end with no error" case.
- **The trace** shows the logger's `>` line first and its `<` line later, when the response `finish` event fires. Middleware can run code before the handler (by running first) and after it (by listening for `finish`), which is how timing, access logging and metrics work.

### What the real Express adds

The core loop above is the same idea. The real library adds a lot of convenience around it:

- **`express.Router()`** creates a mini-application (its own stack) that you mount under a prefix with `app.use("/api", router)`. While inside it, Express strips the prefix from `req.url` and keeps the original in `req.originalUrl`.
- **Body parsers:** `express.json()` and `express.urlencoded()` (bundled since Express 4.16 and kept in 5) read and parse the body, much like our demo parser, plus size limits and content-type checks. In Express 5, `req.body` is `undefined` rather than `{}` when no parser handled the request, so guard for it.
- **Response helpers:** `res.json`, `res.send`, `res.redirect`, `res.cookie`, `res.sendFile`, content negotiation, ETags and caching headers.
- **`express.static`**, `app.set` settings, `trust proxy` (needed to read the real client IP and protocol behind a load balancer), and view engines.
- **Route syntax.** Express 5 uses `path-to-regexp` version 8: bare wildcards must be named (`/files/*splat` rather than `/files/*`), and regular-expression characters in route strings are no longer supported. Express 5 also requires Node.js 18 or later. (These facts come from the Express 5 migration notes as reported by several guides; they were not run in this lesson.)

The same application written against real Express 5 (code **not run** here because the sandbox has no network to install packages; it uses the same API as the mini version):

```javascript
import express from "express";

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());                                   // built-in body parser

app.get("/users/:id", (req, res) => res.json({ id: req.params.id, query: req.query }));
app.post("/users", validateUser, (req, res) => res.status(201).json({ created: req.body.name }));

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {                         // four parameters = error handler
  if (res.headersSent) return next(err);                   // too late to send a JSON error: let Express finish
  res.status(err.status ?? 500).json({ error: err.message });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

## Worked example

Goal: diagnose five real-world Express bugs using the mental model, without running anything:

1. **"My route returns 404 even though I defined it."** Is it registered *after* a layer that already answered (a catch-all `app.use((req, res) => res.status(404)...)` placed too early)? Order is everything. Put the 404 handler last.
2. **"The request hangs forever."** A middleware neither called `next()` nor sent a response (often an `if` branch that forgot the `else`). Every path through a handler must end in exactly one of: send a response, call `next()`, or call `next(err)`.
3. **"My error handler never runs."** Either it has three parameters (so it is treated as a normal handler), or it was registered **before** the routes that fail, so the loop had already passed it. Error handlers go last.
4. **"Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client."** (Node's `ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT`.) Two things both tried to respond: a handler sent a response and then also called `next()`, or `res.json` ran twice (for example after an `if` without `return`). Write `return res.status(400).json(...)` so the function stops.
5. **"`req.body` is undefined."** The body parser is not installed, is registered *after* the route, or the client sent the wrong `Content-Type` so the parser skipped it.

Each diagnosis took one sentence because the model is small: an ordered list, each station either responds, passes on, or raises an alarm.

## In real products

- **Real Express applications** are a short stack of cross-cutting middleware (request ID, logging, security headers, CORS, body parsing, rate limiting, authentication) followed by routers per feature, then a 404 handler and a final error handler that logs the error and returns a safe message.
- **Other Node frameworks use the same model.** Koa and Hono use `async (ctx, next) => { ...; await next(); ... }`, where code after `await next()` runs on the way back, which is the "before and after" trick we did with the `finish` event. Fastify uses hooks and plugins. NestJS is a structured layer over Express or Fastify. Understanding the chain makes all of them easy to read.
- **Other languages have the same idea:** ASP.NET Core's middleware pipeline (`app.Use(...)`), Python's WSGI/ASGI middleware, Go's `http.Handler` wrappers, Java servlet filters.
- **Observability** (Sentry, OpenTelemetry, request logging) plugs in as middleware plus an error handler, which is why order and the four-argument signature matter in production incident debugging.

> [!pitfall] Forgetting that async code needs error plumbing in Express 4. A rejected promise in an `async` handler is **not** forwarded; the request hangs and the process may log an unhandled rejection. In Express 5 rejections are forwarded, but only rejections: a handler that finishes successfully without calling `next()` or sending a response still hangs the request.

> [!pitfall] Registering middleware in the wrong order: authentication after the routes it should protect, `express.json()` after the routes that read `req.body`, CORS after an early response, the error handler before the routes. Top to bottom is the execution order.

> [!pitfall] Doing blocking work inside a handler (heavy JSON parsing, synchronous file reads, CPU loops, `bcrypt` sync calls). Node runs your JavaScript on one thread, so one slow handler delays every other request (see the event-loop lesson). Use async APIs, worker threads or a job queue.

> [!pitfall] Leaking internals in the final error response (stack traces, SQL errors, file paths). Make the error handler return a generic message and an ID, and log the details privately. Do not rely on `NODE_ENV` being set correctly by luck: set it explicitly in production.

> [!tip] Keep handlers thin: parse and validate input in middleware, call a plain function in a service module that knows nothing about `req` and `res`, then format the response. Plain functions are easy to unit test (see the Node testing lesson), and the HTTP layer can be tested with a few requests against a real server on port 0.

> [!tip] Use a small set of conventions: errors carry a `status` and a safe `message`; routes `return` after responding; one error-handling middleware at the end; one request-ID middleware at the top that adds the ID to every log line. Add `app.disable("x-powered-by")` and security headers (for example with `helmet`) as the first lines.

> [!cost] Each middleware adds a little work to every request, and a global body parser reads and parses every body. Mount expensive middleware only where it is needed (`app.use("/api", ...)` or on specific routes), set body size limits, compress responses at the proxy, and measure with a profiler before optimising.

> [!security] The middleware chain is your security perimeter: authentication, authorisation, input validation, rate limiting and body-size limits must run **before** the handler that does the work, and a route accidentally mounted above the guard is unprotected. Test that unauthenticated requests get 401 on every private route, and add the cross-site protections from the browser-security lesson (cookie flags, CSRF defence, CORS allow-list). Keep dependencies patched, since a vulnerable middleware package is a vulnerability in your app.

> [!prod] Production checklist: explicit `NODE_ENV=production`; `trust proxy` configured correctly behind the load balancer; request IDs, structured logs and an error tracker; a single final error handler that hides internals; body size limits and timeouts; rate limiting on authentication routes; health and readiness endpoints; graceful shutdown (stop accepting connections, finish in-flight requests on SIGTERM); a process manager or container restart policy; and dependency auditing in CI.

> [!version] Verified 2026-10-23 in the sandbox on Node.js v22.22.2: the mini-Express and the demo were run and the transcript is real output (the 10 requests were made with Node's built-in `fetch` against a server on a random port). Real Express was NOT installed or run here (the sandbox is offline), so every statement about the real library is from documentation and guides rather than from a run: the forwarding of rejected promises in Express 5 and the rule that resolved promises do not advance the chain, `path-to-regexp` 8 wildcard naming, the Node 18+ requirement and `req.body` being undefined without a parser (confirmed across several third-party migration guides, not the official docs page), the built-in final handler hiding stack traces in production, router prefix stripping, and `res.headersSent`. The linked resources could not be opened from the sandbox.

node-testing.md:
## Why it exists

A test is a small program that runs your code and fails loudly when the result is wrong. Tests are the safety net that lets you change code without fear: after any edit you run one command, and within seconds you know whether you broke something. For many years a JavaScript project needed a third-party test framework (Jest, Mocha, Vitest) before it could have a single test. **Modern Node.js ships its own test runner**, `node:test`, plus an assertion library, mocks and fake timers, so you can start testing a project with zero dependencies: nothing to install, nothing to configure, and no supply-chain surface.

This lesson builds a realistic mini test-suite for four kinds of code: a pure function, a class with an external dependency, time-based code, and an HTTP server. Along the way it shows the habits that make code easy to test, since testability is mostly a design question. (The Testing strategy lesson covers the bigger picture: the test pyramid, test doubles, flakiness.)

**Mental model:** each test is **arrange, act, assert**: set up the situation, do the one thing under test, check the outcome. A good test is small, fast, independent of other tests, and fails for exactly one reason that its name explains.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

All of the code and output below was run with Node.js v22.22.2. No packages were installed.

### The code under test

```javascript
export function slugify(text) {
  if (typeof text !== "string") throw new TypeError("slugify expects a string");
  return text
    .normalize("NFKD")
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, "")          // strip accents
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-")
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, "");
}
```

```javascript
// The price lookup is passed in, so tests can replace it with a fake.
export class Cart {
  constructor(fetchPrice) {
    this.fetchPrice = fetchPrice;
    this.items = [];
  }
  add(sku, qty = 1) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(qty) || qty < 1) throw new RangeError("qty must be a positive integer");
    this.items.push({ sku, qty });
  }
  async total() {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const { sku, qty } of this.items) sum += (await this.fetchPrice(sku)) * qty;
    return Math.round(sum * 100) / 100;
  }
}
```

```javascript
export function debounce(fn, ms) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}
```

```javascript
import http from "node:http";

export function createServer() {
  return http.createServer((req, res) => {
    const url = new URL(req.url, "http://localhost");
    if (url.pathname === "/health") {
      res.setHeader("content-type", "application/json");
      return res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: "ok" }));
    }
    if (url.pathname === "/sum") {
      const a = Number(url.searchParams.get("a"));
      const b = Number(url.searchParams.get("b"));
      if (Number.isNaN(a) || Number.isNaN(b)) {
        res.statusCode = 400;
        return res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: "a and b must be numbers" }));
      }
      return res.end(JSON.stringify({ sum: a + b }));
    }
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.end("not found");
  });
}
```

Notice two design choices. `Cart` receives its price lookup **as a constructor argument** instead of importing a real network client, so a test can pass a fake (this is **dependency injection**, and it is the single most useful testing technique). And `createServer` **returns** the server instead of calling `listen` itself, so a test can start it on any free port.

### The test API

`node:test` gives you `test` (or `it`), `describe` for grouping, hooks (`before`, `after`, `beforeEach`, `afterEach`), and `mock` for fakes. Assertions come from `node:assert/strict`, which compares with strict equality and prints readable diffs. A test **passes if its function returns (or its promise resolves) without throwing**, and fails if it throws or rejects. A first test file, including one assertion that is deliberately wrong:

```javascript
import { describe, it } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
